//! A mutex and a bounded channel, for tasks that share data.
//!
//! A `Mutex` guards a value so that one task at a time works on it: `lock`
//! returns a `MutexGuard`, which reads and replaces the value until it is
//! released. `channel` makes a `Channel`, a queue holding at most a fixed
//! number of values, which tasks fill through a `Sender` and empty through a
//! `Receiver`, in the order the values were sent. Every module sees these
//! names without an import, and a module's own definition of one of them
//! shadows the one here.
//!
//! Tasks take turns on one thread, so a task is interrupted only where it
//! awaits; a mutex is for a value that must stay consistent across an
//! `.await`. Copying a `Mutex`, `MutexGuard`, `Channel`, `Sender` or
//! `Receiver`, or passing it to a function, shares it rather than copying it.
//! The methods that lock, set, release, send, receive or close take
//! `mut self`, and need a mutable receiver, such as one held in a `mut`
//! binding.
//!
//! A task waiting for a lock, for room in a channel or for a value yields to
//! the other tasks and tries again, over and over: it keeps the processor
//! busy while it waits, and a wait that can never end, such as for a lock
//! that is never released, goes on forever rather than being reported as a
//! deadlock.
//!
//! A producer task sends values through a channel and closes it; the
//! receiver takes them until `recv` gives `None`:
//!
//! ```
//! async fn produce(mut tx: Sender<Int>) {
//!     for i in 1..4 { tx.send(i * 10).await }
//!     tx.close() // once the channel is empty, `recv` gives `None`
//! }
//!
//! async fn main() {
//!     let ch: Channel<Int> = channel(2)
//!     spawn(produce(ch.sender()))
//!     let mut rx = ch.receiver()
//!     let mut total = 0
//!     while true {
//!         match rx.recv().await {
//!             Some(v) => total = total + v
//!             None => break
//!         }
//!     }
//!     assert_eq(total, 60)
//! }
//! ```

// Nova standard library -- synchronisation.
//
// An *async* mutex, which is the primitive a cooperative executor needs. A
// thread mutex would be pointless here: `crates/nova-runtime/src/task.rs`'s
// first line is "A single-threaded cooperative executor". But a task can be
// suspended in the middle of a critical section at any `.await`, and while it
// is suspended another task runs -- so anything whose invariant spans a
// suspension point needs exactly this.
//
// Contention is handled by yielding and retrying rather than by parking. That
// keeps the whole module in Nova with no runtime change, and the cost is
// stated rather than hidden: a waiter stays *runnable*, so `report_deadlock`
// cannot see it and a never-released lock spins instead of being diagnosed.
// See the design doc's §5 for the condition that would justify revisiting.

/// A value of type `T` that one task at a time can work on, through a
/// `MutexGuard`.
///
/// `lock` and `try_lock` return a guard, and the guard's `release` unlocks
/// the mutex. Nothing releases a guard for you: one never released leaves the
/// mutex locked for the rest of the program. A mutex is not re-entrant: a
/// task that locks one it already holds waits forever. It makes no fairness
/// guarantee either: when several tasks wait, which one gets the lock next is
/// unspecified.
pub record Mutex<T> { locked: Bool, value: T }

// The right to touch a `Mutex`'s value, and the obligation to release it.
//
// NOT RAII, and not by choice: `Drop` is described in three spec files
// (`12-TYPESYSTEM.md`, `13-RUNTIME.md`, `14-CODEGEN.md`) and implemented in
// none of them -- no handling in nova-typeck, nova-resolver or nova-mir, and
// no `trait Drop` in std/core. Releasing this guard is pure Nova
// (`self.owner.locked = false`), so RAII here would need that *language*
// feature; nothing in the runtime substitutes for it.
//
// ADR 0012 is the precedent for what to do instead, not the reason there is
// no RAII: it chose explicit, idempotent `close` for `File` over a
// collector-based backstop, and accepted a uniform documented leak. So
// release is explicit and idempotent here too, and a guard that is never
// released leaves the mutex locked for the life of the process -- the same
// trade, accepted for the same reason.
//
// (An earlier version of this comment claimed ADR 0012 shows the collector
// "offers no per-object hook". When written, ADR 0012 showed the opposite:
// the hook existed and the ADR named it, and its argument was that the hook
// reported *the dying object's own address*, which tells an `fd`-keyed table
// nothing. Since 2026-10-01 the collector no longer has a per-object hook at
// all (ADR 0020). Either way the argument is about `File`, not about a
// pure-Nova guard, and does not transfer here.)
//
// `released` is what makes that idempotence true rather than merely claimed,
// and `File`'s precedent does not supply it. `File`'s second `close` is safe
// because an `fd` is a key into a runtime table and absence from that table
// *is* closedness (ADR 0012). A guard has no table and no key: `owner` is the
// live mutex itself, because records are reference types
// (`docs/adr/0005-mutable-receivers-and-one-shot-hash.md`), and this compiler
// has no move or linear checking, so a guard stays usable after `release`
// (`crates/nova-cli/tests/run_tests.rs:3916-3919` records the grep behind
// that). An unconditional `self.owner.locked = false` would therefore let a
// stale guard clear `locked` while a *different* task is inside its critical
// section. The flag supplies the missing state directly, so `release` and
// `set` are both no-ops on a released guard -- see both below, and
// `tests/runtime/sync_mutex_stale_guard_cannot_steal.nova` and
// `sync_mutex_stale_guard_cannot_write.nova`, one fixture per use.
//
// Inert against mistakes, not against tampering, and the reason is one fact:
// **Nova enforces no field privacy.** Every hole below follows from it.
// `released` is an ordinary readable, writable field, so `g.released = false`
// on a guard that was genuinely obtained and genuinely released resurrects it,
// and its next `release` frees a lock a different task now holds -- measured.
// That needs nothing but the stale guard itself, which is a *strictly weaker*
// precondition than building `MutexGuard { owner: m, released: false }` from
// scratch (also legal, also measured, and it needs a `Mutex<T>` to name). The
// same fact is why `File { fd: 9999 }` is constructible
// (`docs/adr/0012-file-descriptor-lifecycle.md`), with one difference worth
// stating: a forged `fd` safely misses a table lookup, whereas a forged or
// resurrected guard writes straight to the live mutex. Documented, not
// enforced. `get` is not guarded by the flag at all, deliberately, and gives
// its own reason below.
/// The right to work on a locked `Mutex`'s value, and the duty to release
/// it.
///
/// `get` reads the value and `set` replaces it. Call `release` when done: the
/// mutex stays locked until then. After `release`, `set` and a second
/// `release` do nothing, though `get` still reads the value, unguarded.
pub record MutexGuard<T> { owner: Mutex<T>, released: Bool }

impl<T> Mutex<T> {
    /// Returns an unlocked mutex holding `value`.
    pub fn new(value: T) -> Mutex<T> { Mutex { locked: false, value: value } }

    // Take the flag if it is free. Private: the two public entry points differ
    // only in what they do when it is not, and returning a `Bool` is what lets
    // `lock`'s `while` condition read the result directly -- `!self.take()`
    // rather than an `Option` the loop would have to inspect and discard.
    //
    // (An earlier version of this comment gave the reason as `Option` having
    // "no `unwrap`". That is false: `Option::unwrap` is at
    // `std/core/lib.nova:89` and std/core calls it six times, on five lines
    // -- 490, 520, 538, 601 and 637, the last of which calls it twice.)
    fn take(mut self) -> Bool {
        if self.locked { return false }
        self.locked = true
        true
    }

    // Take the lock, or report that it is held. The caller that gets `Some`
    // receives the guard it needs; the caller that gets `None` has nothing it
    // could mistake for one.
    /// Locks the mutex and returns a guard if it is free, or returns `None`
    /// if it is locked, even by the calling task.
    ///
    /// It is not `async`, and never waits.
    pub fn try_lock(mut self) -> Option<MutexGuard<T>> {
        if self.take() { Some(MutexGuard { owner: self, released: false }) } else { None }
    }

    // Take the lock, yielding to every other runnable task until it is free.
    //
    // `while`, not `loop`: Nova has no `loop` keyword -- `loop {` parses as a
    // record literal with `loop` as the type name. `yield_now` re-queues this
    // task behind everything currently runnable, so the holder always gets a
    // turn and can always release; which *waiter* wins afterwards is
    // unspecified, and this mutex makes no fairness guarantee.
    /// Locks the mutex, waiting while it is locked, and returns a guard.
    ///
    /// An `async fn`. If the mutex is free, it locks it without suspending;
    /// otherwise the calling task yields to the other tasks and tries again
    /// until the mutex is released. The wait keeps the processor busy, and
    /// goes on forever, with no deadlock reported, if the mutex is never
    /// released, as when the calling task already holds it.
    ///
    /// Two tasks adding to one total, each holding the lock across a
    /// suspension:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// async fn add(mut total: Mutex<Int>, n: Int) {
    ///     let mut guard = total.lock().await
    ///     let before = guard.get()
    ///     yield_now().await // other tasks run here, but none can lock `total`
    ///     guard.set(before + n)
    ///     guard.release() // nothing releases a guard for you
    /// }
    ///
    /// async fn main() {
    ///     let mut total = Mutex::new(0)
    ///     let a = spawn(add(total, 1))
    ///     let b = spawn(add(total, 2))
    ///     a.join().await
    ///     b.join().await
    ///     assert_eq(total.try_lock().unwrap().get(), 3)
    /// }
    /// ```
    pub async fn lock(mut self) -> MutexGuard<T> {
        while !self.take() { yield_now().await }
        MutexGuard { owner: self, released: false }
    }
}

impl<T> MutexGuard<T> {
    // The guarded value. Records and arrays are reference types, so mutating
    // the returned value's fields or elements mutates what the mutex holds --
    // which is the point. But a `T` with no assignable interior is *copied*
    // out, and mutating that copy cannot reach the mutex: `Int`, `Bool`,
    // `Float` and `Char` have no fields to assign, and `std/strings` has no
    // in-place mutation at all (zero `mut self` methods in its nineteen
    // `pub fn`s). Use `set` for those.
    //
    // Callable after `release`, and unlike `release` and `set` this
    // deliberately does *not* return early on `released`. The guard now
    // carries the state to detect the case, so the earlier reason -- that the
    // language cannot prevent it -- is no longer the reason: the library could
    // prevent it and chooses not to. There is nothing to return instead. `get`
    // yields a `T`, `T` is generic, and no default exists to invent for it, so
    // an early return has no value to hand back. The two alternatives are a
    // signature change to `Option<T>`, which every caller would then unwrap,
    // and a panic -- and a Nova panic aborts the process rather than unwinding,
    // because nothing unwinds across a poll boundary. A stale read is also the
    // harmless one of the three: it cannot corrupt the mutex, where a stale
    // `set` or `release` writes to it. Documented, not enforced.
    /// Returns the guarded value.
    ///
    /// A record or an array is shared rather than copied, so a change to its
    /// fields or elements changes the value the mutex holds. A value with
    /// nothing to change in place, such as an `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`, `Char`
    /// or `String`, comes out as a copy: replace it with `set`. `get` still
    /// answers after `release`, though the value is then unguarded.
    pub fn get(self) -> T { self.owner.value }

    // Replace the guarded value -- the other half of the pair `Vec<T>` already
    // ships, `get` at `std/collections/lib.nova:117` with `set` at `:125`, and
    // for the same reason: without it a `Mutex<Int>` is a read-only view.
    //
    // A no-op on a released guard, exactly as `release` is. A stale `set` is
    // the worse of the two hazards `released` closes: a stale `release` frees
    // a flag another task owns, but a stale `set` writes into the protected
    // value while that task is inside its critical section. Pinned by
    // `tests/runtime/sync_mutex_stale_guard_cannot_write.nova`; deleting the
    // line below fails that fixture and no other.
    /// Replaces the guarded value with `v`.
    ///
    /// Does nothing if the guard has been released.
    pub fn set(mut self, v: T) {
        if self.released { return }
        self.owner.value = v
    }

    // Release the lock. Idempotent -- a second call is a no-op, and `released`
    // makes that true rather than only stated: without it this body is an
    // unconditional write that would free a lock the caller no longer owns.
    /// Unlocks the mutex, so another task can lock it.
    ///
    /// Releasing the same guard again does nothing.
    pub fn release(mut self) {
        if self.released { return }
        self.released = true
        self.owner.locked = false
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ channel

// A fixed-capacity FIFO over a flat array. Private: it exists only to give
// `Channel` its buffer, and `std/collections` has no `Queue` to borrow --
// `Vec::pop` takes from the end, which is LIFO.
//
// `head` is the index of the next value out; `len` is how many are live.
// Capacity lives on `Channel` rather than here, because `data` starts empty
// and so cannot report it until the first send allocates.
//
// INVARIANT: `head >= 0`, `len >= 0`, `cap >= 1`. Every `%` below therefore
// has non-negative operands, which matters because Nova's `%` is a
// TRUNCATING remainder -- `-7 % 3` is `-1`, not `2`. Any operation added
// later that seeks backwards must re-establish this, or the wrap arithmetic
// lands outside the array. `std/core/lib.nova:285` and
// `std/collections/lib.nova:203` carry the same warning for hash bucketing.
record Ring<T> { data: [T], head: Int, len: Int }

/// A first-in, first-out queue of values of type `T` that holds at most a
/// fixed number of them, from `channel`.
///
/// Values go in through a `Sender` and come out through a `Receiver`, in the
/// order they were sent. `sender` and `receiver` make as many of each as are
/// needed, all sharing the one channel. Once a sender closes it, no more
/// values go in, but those already in it can still be received.
pub record Channel<T> { ring: Ring<T>, cap: Int, closed: Bool }

// Two views onto one channel. Records are reference types, so both observe
// every mutation; the split carries intent -- hand `tx` to producers and `rx`
// to consumers -- and not enforcement. Nova enforces no field privacy, and
// that is unenforceable rather than merely unenforced: a field's `vis`
// survives the AST (`crates/nova-ast/src/item.rs:71`) and is dropped at
// AST->HIR lowering (`crates/nova-hir/src/lib.rs:918-923`), so
// `check_field_set` (`crates/nova-typeck/src/check.rs:6214-6272`) has no
// visibility left to check. Three routes, of very unequal reach:
//
// - Forging `Sender { ch: c }` grants NOTHING. The literal needs a
//   `Channel<T>` to name, and `sender()`/`receiver()` below are `pub` and
//   take a plain `self`, so whoever can name the channel can already obtain
//   both handles legitimately.
// - `ch.closed = false` REOPENS a closed channel, from any legitimately-held
//   handle, and destroys the one property that gives a consumer loop a
//   termination condition: that `recv`'s `None` means closed AND drained.
//   `close` only ever sets the flag true, so no API path reaches this -- the
//   field is simply ordinary. This is the strongest of the three.
// - Writing `ch.ring.head` or `ch.ring.len` breaks the INVARIANT above that
//   the truncating `%` depends on, and reaches it without ever naming the
//   non-`pub` `Ring<T>`.
//
// Documented, not prevented, as the standing `MutexGuard` is -- and ranked
// the same way, in `nova-spec/20-STDLIB.md` section 13, under the paragraph
// beginning "Neither flag reaches tampering", where naming forgery alone
// was corrected on 2026-08-21 for understating the reach. An earlier
// version of this comment made exactly that mistake here; corrected
// 2026-08-22. Cited by its opening words, and its LINE NUMBER IS
// DELIBERATELY NOT RECORDED, because it moves: every number this citation
// has ever carried has been wrong, including the one it was written with.
// Grep the quoted phrase.
/// The sending side of a `Channel`, from `Channel::sender`.
///
/// Every `Sender` of a channel sends into the same queue, and `close` on any
/// of them closes the channel for all.
pub record Sender<T> { ch: Channel<T> }

/// The receiving side of a `Channel`, from `Channel::receiver`.
///
/// Every `Receiver` of a channel takes from the same queue, so each value is
/// received once, by whichever receiver takes it first.
pub record Receiver<T> { ch: Channel<T> }

// A bounded channel holding at most `buffer` values.
//
// `buffer < 1` clamps to 1. A zero-capacity rendezvous channel needs a
// handoff protocol that yield-and-retry cannot express without a second wait
// state, and clamping follows `Int::pad`'s precedent of an early return over
// a panic.
//
// The call site must annotate: `let ch: Channel<Int> = channel(2)`. The type
// parameter appears only in the return type, and Nova has no turbofish --
// `channel<Int>(2)` is a parse error, read as two comparisons.
/// Returns a new channel, open and empty, that holds at most `buffer` values.
///
/// A `buffer` below 1 counts as 1. Annotate the binding with the value type,
/// as in `let ch: Channel<Int> = channel(8)`: nothing in the call names it.
/// Room for all `buffer` values is allocated by the first send.
pub fn channel<T>(buffer: Int) -> Channel<T> {
    let cap = if buffer < 1 { 1 } else { buffer }
    Channel { ring: Ring { data: [], head: 0, len: 0 }, cap: cap, closed: false }
}

impl<T> Channel<T> {
    /// Returns a `Sender` for the channel.
    pub fn sender(self) -> Sender<T> { Sender { ch: self } }

    /// Returns a `Receiver` for the channel.
    pub fn receiver(self) -> Receiver<T> { Receiver { ch: self } }

    // Enqueue, or report refusal. Private: `Sender::try_send` is the public
    // door. Returns false when closed and when full, which is why the async
    // `send` must re-check `closed` itself to tell the two apart.
    fn push(mut self, v: T) -> Bool {
        if self.closed { return false }
        if self.ring.len == self.cap { return false }
        if self.ring.data.len() == 0 {
            // First send. `[fill; n]` needs a value of `T` and a fresh
            // channel has none, so allocation waits for one -- the same trick
            // `Vec::push` uses to grow. Every slot holds `v` until
            // overwritten, so the channel briefly retains up to `cap`
            // references to the first value sent.
            self.ring.data = [v; self.cap]
            self.ring.len = 1
            return true
        }
        self.ring.data[(self.ring.head + self.ring.len) % self.cap] = v
        self.ring.len = self.ring.len + 1
        true
    }

    // Dequeue, or None when empty. Says nothing about `closed` -- that
    // distinction belongs to `Receiver::recv`.
    fn pop(mut self) -> Option<T> {
        if self.ring.len == 0 { return None }
        let v = self.ring.data[self.ring.head]
        self.ring.head = (self.ring.head + 1) % self.cap
        self.ring.len = self.ring.len - 1
        Some(v)
    }
}

impl<T> Sender<T> {
    // Send if there is room. False when the channel is full OR closed; a
    // caller that needs to distinguish them should use `send`, which returns
    // false only for closed.
    /// Sends `v` and returns `true` if the channel has room, or returns
    /// `false` without sending it if the channel is full or closed.
    ///
    /// It is not `async`, and never waits. To tell a full channel from a
    /// closed one, use `send`.
    pub fn try_send(mut self, v: T) -> Bool { self.ch.push(v) }

    // Refuse all further sends. Buffered values stay readable, so a producer
    // may close the instant it stops producing and the consumer still drains.
    // Idempotent: a second call sets an already-set flag.
    /// Closes the channel: later sends, through any sender, return `false`.
    ///
    /// Values already in the channel stay there for the receivers, whose
    /// `recv` returns `None` once they are all taken. Closing a closed channel
    /// does nothing. Nothing closes a channel for you: close it when done
    /// sending, or a receiver waiting in `recv` waits forever.
    pub fn close(mut self) { self.ch.closed = true }

    // Send, waiting while the channel is full. Returns false only when the
    // channel is closed -- unlike `try_send`, which also returns false when
    // merely full.
    //
    // Waiting is `yield_now`, not parking, which keeps this module in Nova
    // with no executor change (ADR 0016 made the same call for `Mutex`). The
    // cost is stated rather than hidden: a waiter stays *runnable*, so
    // `report_deadlock` cannot see it, and a producer blocked on a channel
    // nobody drains spins instead of being diagnosed.
    /// Sends `v`, waiting while the channel is full, and returns `true` once
    /// it is in, or `false`, without sending it, if the channel is closed.
    ///
    /// An `async fn`. If the channel has room, it sends without suspending;
    /// otherwise the calling task yields to the other tasks and tries again
    /// until there is room or the channel is closed. The wait keeps the
    /// processor busy, and goes on forever, with no deadlock reported, if no
    /// task ever takes a value out or closes the channel.
    pub async fn send(mut self, v: T) -> Bool {
        while !self.ch.push(v) {
            if self.ch.closed { return false }
            yield_now().await
        }
        true
    }
}

impl<T> Receiver<T> {
    // Take a value if one is buffered. None means empty, and says nothing
    // about whether more will arrive -- `recv` is what distinguishes those.
    /// Takes the oldest value in the channel, or returns `None` if it is
    /// empty, whether or not it is closed.
    ///
    /// It is not `async`, and never waits.
    pub fn try_recv(mut self) -> Option<T> { self.ch.pop() }

    // Take the next value, waiting while the channel is empty and open.
    //
    // None means closed AND drained, never merely empty. That is the whole
    // reason `close` is explicit: `Drop` is specified in three spec files and
    // implemented in none, so nothing closes a channel on the producer's
    // behalf, and without this distinction a consumer loop could not
    // terminate.
    //
    // Buffered values are drained before the close is reported, because `pop`
    // is tried before `closed` is read on every iteration.
    /// Takes the oldest value in the channel, waiting while it is empty and
    /// open, or returns `None` once it is closed and empty.
    ///
    /// An `async fn`. If a value is there, it takes it without suspending;
    /// otherwise the calling task yields to the other tasks and tries again.
    /// The values still in a closed channel are received before `None`. The
    /// wait keeps the processor busy, and goes on forever, with no deadlock
    /// reported, if no task ever sends a value or closes the channel.
    pub async fn recv(mut self) -> Option<T> {
        let mut out = self.ch.pop()
        while out.is_none() {
            if self.ch.closed { return None }
            yield_now().await
            out = self.ch.pop()
        }
        out
    }
}
